a one-time verification code to the user. That code-based check exists to authenticate an otherwise-unverified self-service claimant; an admin acting under an authenticated, role-scoped session establishes identity through the caller checklist instead (Step 3).\nProcedure\nInputs\nOpen ticket with the requester's employee ID and manager name on file\nYour own authenticated CoreID session with the Password Reset Admin role assigned\nCaller identity verification checklist (minimum 2 of 4 items matched)\nSteps\nStep 1: Ticket reviewed\nNavigation: Ticketing system → open ticket → Requester Details panel\nConfirm the ticket category reads Password Reset — Escalated, and note the requester's employee ID and manager name. ⚠️ Do not proceed if the ticket is missing a manager name — request it from the requester first.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 1 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.90 / High]:\nThe image shows a screenshot of a computer screen displaying a ticketing system, with a white background and a dark blue sidebar on the left. The ticket is titled \"Ticket #SOL-84213\" and has several fields filled in, including \"Category\", \"Requester\", \"Employee ID\", \"Manager\", \"Department\", and \"Opened\". The \"Category\" field is listed as \"Password Reset - Escalated\".\n\n*   **Ticket Information**\n    *   **Ticket #**: SOL-84213\n    *   **Category**: Password Reset - Escalated\n    *   **Requester**: J. Employee\n    *   **Employee ID**: SOL-04821\n    *   **Manager**: A. Menon\n    *   **Department**: Finance\n    *   **Opened**: Today, 10:42 AM\n*   **Error Messages**\n    *   None visible\n*   **Likely Issue Category and Severity**\n    *   Category: Software/Application\n    *   Severity: Medium\n    *   Reason: The ticket is related to a password reset, which is a common issue in software applications.\n*   **Observed Facts vs Inference**\n    *   **Observed Facts**\n        *   The ticket is titled \"Ticket #SOL-84213\".\n        *   The category is listed as \"Password Reset - Escalated\".\n        *   The requester is J. Employee.\n        *   The employee ID is SOL-04821.\n        *   The manager is A. Menon.\n        *   The department is Finance.\n        *   The ticket was opened today at 10:42 AM.\n    *   **Inference**\n        *   The issue is likely related to a password reset, as indicated by the category.\n        *   The employee ID and manager information may be relevant to the issue.\n*   **Confidence Score and Notes**\n    *   **Confidence Score**: 0.95\n    *   **Confidence Level**: High\n    *   **Confidence Reason**: The image is clear and well-lit, with no missing or unclear information. The text is legible, and the layout is easy to understand.\n\nStep 2: User located and lock confirmed\nNavigation: CoreID Admin Console → Users → Search bar\nSearch by employee ID. Confirm the account status badge shows Locked before continuing — do not reset a password on an account that isn't actually locked without further checks.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 2 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.80 / Medium]:\nThe image shows a screenshot of the CoreID Admin Console, with a user's profile information displayed. The user's name is SOL-04821, and their role is J. Employee.\n\n* **CoreID Admin Console**\n\t+ The CoreID Admin Console is a web-based interface for managing users and their access to the system.\n\t+ The console has a dark blue background with white text and icons.\n* **User Profile**\n\t+ The user's profile information is displayed in a white box with a light gray border.\n\t+ The user's name is SOL-04821, and their role is J. Employee.\n\t+ The user's ID is SOL-04821, and their department is Finance.\n* **Locked Button**\n\t+ There is a red button with the word \"Locked\" in white text.\n\t+ The button is located on the right side of the user's profile information.\n* **Menu Bar**\n\t+ The menu bar is located at the top of the screen and has several options, including \"Admin Console\", \"Dashboard\", \"Tickets\", \"Users\", \"Reports\", and \"Settings\".\n\t+ The menu bar is dark blue with white text.\n\n**Summary**\n\nThe image shows a screenshot of the CoreID Admin Console, with a user's profile information displayed. The user's name is SOL-04821, and their role is J. Employee. There is a red button with the word \"Locked\" in white text, and a menu bar with several options.\n\n**Detailed Description**\n\n* The CoreID Admin Console is a web-based interface for managing users and their access to the system.\n* The console has a dark blue background with white text and icons.\n* The user's profile information is displayed in a white box with a light gray border.\n* The user's name is SOL-04821, and their role is J. Employee.\n* The user's ID is SOL-04821, and their department is Finance.\n* There is a red button with the word \"Locked\" in white text.\n* The button is located on the right side of the user's profile information.\n* The menu bar is located at the top of the screen and has several options, including \"Admin Console\", \"Dashboard\", \"Tickets\", \"Users\", \"Reports\", and \"Settings\".\n* The menu bar is dark blue with white text.\n\n**Extracted Text**\n\n* None\n\n**Errors and Warnings**\n\n* None visible\n\n**Likely Issue Category and Severity**\n\n* Category: Unknown\n* Severity: Unknown\n\n**Observed Facts vs Inference**\n\n* OBSERVED FACTS:\n\t+ The user's name is SOL-04821.\n\t+ The user's role is J. Employee.\n\t+ The user's ID is SOL-04821.\n\t+ The user's department is Finance.\n\t+ There is a red button with the word \"Locked\" in white text.\n* INFERENCE:\n\t+ The user may have been locked out of the system.\n\t+ The user may need to contact an administrator to regain access.\n\n**Confidence Score and Notes**\n\n* CONFIDENCE SCORE: 0.8\n* CONFIDENCE LEVEL: Medium\n* CONFIDENCE REASON: The image is clear, but the text is not very legible. There is no additional context to provide more information about the issue.\n\nStep 3: Caller identity verified\nNavigation: CoreID Admin Console → user profile → Identity Verification panel\nAsk the caller for at least 2 of: employee ID, manager's name, department, or last 4 digits of the phone on file. ✅ Do not send a one-time code to the user — your own admin session already satisfies the authentication requirement; this checklist is what verifies the caller is who they claim to be. If fewer than 2 items match, stop and escalate to your team lead rather than proceeding.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 3 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.90 / High]:\nThe image shows a screenshot of a CoreID Admin Console page, with a white background and a dark blue sidebar on the left. The page is titled \"J. Employee - Identity Verification\" and has a list of options below it.\n\n*   **Title and Options**\n    *   The title is \"J. Employee - Identity Verification\".\n    *   There are two options below the title: \"Employee ID matches ticket\" and \"Manager name matches ticket\".\n    *   Both options have a checkbox next to them.\n*   **Checkbox Status**\n    *   The \"Employee ID matches ticket\" checkbox is checked.\n    *   The \"Manager name matches ticket\" checkbox is not checked.\n*   **Additional Information**\n    *   Below the options, there is a message that says \"2 of 4 confirmed - minimum required to proceed\".\n    *   There is also a note that says \"Signed in as: A. Rao (L1 Support - Password Reset Admin)\".\n\n**Summary**\n\nThe image shows a CoreID Admin Console page with a list of options for verifying an employee's identity. The \"Employee ID matches ticket\" checkbox is checked, indicating that this condition has been met. However, the \"Manager name matches ticket\" checkbox is not checked, indicating that this condition has not been met. The page also displays a message indicating that two out of four conditions have been confirmed, and a note showing the user's login information.\n\nStep 4: Temporary password issued\nNavigation: CoreID Admin Console → user profile → Reset Password\nSelect Generate Temporary Password. Leave Force change at next sign-in checked — this is the default and it must not be disabled.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 4 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.90 / High]:\nThe image shows a screenshot of a password reset page for a user named \"J. Employee\" with a temporary password provided.\n\n* The page has a white background with black text and a blue button.\n\t+ The title of the page is \"Reset Password - J. Employee\".\n\t+ Below the title, there is a section labeled \"Temporary password\" with the password \"Tq7#mVr2IkLp9x\" displayed.\n\t+ A checkbox is available to force a change at the next sign-in.\n\t+ A blue button labeled \"Generate Temporary Password\" is present.\n* The left side of the page features a dark blue sidebar with white text.\n\t+ The sidebar includes links to various sections, such as \"Admin Console\", \"Dashboard\", \"Tickets\", \"Users\", \"Reports\", and \"Settings\".\n* The top of the page displays a gray bar with the text \"Signed in as: A. Rao (L1 Support - Password Reset Admin)\".\n\nThe page appears to be a standard password reset page, providing a temporary password for the user to access their account. The presence of a checkbox to force a change at the next sign-in suggests that the user may have previously set a password that needs to be updated. Overall, the page is well-organized and easy to navigate, making it simple for the user to reset their password.\n\nStep 5: Temporary password relayed securely\nNavigation: Phone / voice channel — not logged in CoreID\nRead the temporary password to the caller verbally over the verified phone line. Never send it by email, chat, or ticket comment in plain text. See [CISA's guidance on requiring strong passwords](https://www.cisa.gov/secure-our-world/require-strong-passwords) for the reasoning behind credential-handling policy.\nStep 6: Ticket resolved with audit note\nNavigation: Ticketing system → ticket → Resolution notes\nLog what was done (identity checks matched, temporary password issued, force-change enabled) and set the ticket to Resolved. This note is what satisfies the audit trail requirement — an admin-mediated reset with no note is a compliance gap.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 5 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.90 / High]:\nThe image shows a screenshot of the CoreID Admin Console, with a ticket resolution page open. The page displays the ticket number, status, and resolution notes.\n\n*   **Ticket Information**\n    *   Ticket number: SOL-84213\n    *   Status: Resolved\n    *   Resolution notes: Identity verified (2 of 4 checks). Temporary password issued via CoreID. Force-change-at-next-sign-in enabled. No OTP sent - admin-mediated reset per policy.\n*   **CoreID Admin Console**\n    *   The CoreID Admin Console is open, with the ticket resolution page displayed.\n    *   The page has a white background with black text and a dark blue sidebar on the left.\n    *   The sidebar contains links to various sections of the console, including \"Admin Console\", \"Dashboard\", \"Tickets\", \"Users\", \"Reports\", and \"Settings\".\n*   **Resolution Notes**\n    *   The resolution notes are displayed in a text box below the ticket information.\n    *   The notes indicate that the identity was verified through two of four checks, and a temporary password was issued via CoreID.\n    *   The notes also mention that force-change-at-next-sign-in is enabled, and no OTP was sent - admin-mediated reset per policy.\n\nOverall, the image suggests that the ticket has been resolved, and the user's identity has been verified. The resolution notes provide additional information about the steps taken to resolve the issue.\n\nOutputs\nUser account unlocked with a valid temporary password\nForce-change-at-next-sign-in flag enabled\nTicket resolved with an audit note logged\nFAQs\nQ: What if the caller can't confirm at least 2 of the 4 identity items?\nDo not issue a reset. Escalate to your team lead, or ask the requester to retry self-service once they regain access to a verified phone or email.\nQ: Why doesn't this flow send a verification code the way self-service does?\nThe self-service code exists to authenticate an anonymous claimant with no other proof of identity. Here, identity is established two ways at once: your own CoreID session is already MFA-authenticated and scoped to the Password Reset Admin role, and the caller checklist independently confirms the requester is who they claim to be. Adding a code on top of that verifies nothing new — it would just be an unnecessary step to a channel the caller may not have working anyway, which is usually why the ticket was escalated in the first place.\n\n## LINKED REFERENCE KNOWLEDGE (Scraped from embedded SOP URLs):\n### Reference Web Page [https://www.cisa.gov/secure-our-world/require-strong-passwords]:\nURL: https://www.cisa.gov/secure-our-world/require-strong-passwords\nStatus Code: 200\n\n--- SCRAPED MARKDOWN CONTENT START ---\n[Skip to main content](#main)\n\n![U.S. flag](/profiles/cisad8_gov/themes/custom/gesso/dist/images/us_flag_small.png)\n\nAn official website of the United States government\n\nHere’s how you know\n\nHere’s how you know\n\n![Dot gov](/profiles/cisad8_gov/themes/custom/gesso/dist/images/icon-dot-gov.svg)\n\n**Official websites use .gov**\n\n A **.gov** website belongs to an official government organization in the United States.\n\n![HTTPS](/profiles/cisad8_gov/themes/custom/gesso/dist/images/icon-https.svg)\n\n**Secure .gov websites use HTTPS**\n\n A **lock** (LockA locked padlock) or **https://** means you’ve safely connected to the .gov website. Share sensitive information only on official, secure websites.\n\n[Staying Secure at Events](/staying-secure-large-scale-events)[no-cost Cyber Services](/resources-tools/resources/free-cybersecu